    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0517" n="505" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg505"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/27729: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-03T16:00:00+03:00">February 3, 1943—4 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 4—11:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>103. A neutral colleague has informed me that a member of the Bulgarian Legation
                    here recently told him that while the strong Russo-phile sentiment in Bulgaria
                    would not permit the sending of Bulgarian troops to aid the Germans on the
                    Russian front, the Bulgarian Army would as a matter of course fight side by side
                    with the Axis forces in the event of the opening of a second front anywhere else
                    on the Continent.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
